WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.036 --> 00:00:01.290
(ska music)

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:05.181
- People sometimes here in
the US think ska bands died.

00:00:05.181 --> 00:00:08.151
But it grew in other parts of the world.

00:00:08.151 --> 00:00:10.946
And then it leaked back
into the Latin ska bands

00:00:10.946 --> 00:00:12.606
that we have in L.A.

00:00:12.606 --> 00:00:15.692
(fast ska music)

00:00:15.692 --> 00:00:18.041
Most people in the rest
of the world don't expect

00:00:18.041 --> 00:00:20.878
Latinos in L.A. to listen to ska music,

00:00:20.878 --> 00:00:23.419
or even know about it,
but it's what we do.

00:00:23.419 --> 00:00:27.586
(ska music ends)
(applause and cheers)

00:00:29.056 --> 00:00:30.456
(latin music)

00:00:30.456 --> 00:00:33.493
My name's Clemente Ruiz,
I'm a musician, producer.

00:00:33.493 --> 00:00:35.959
I'm also the guy behind
the biggest ska punk

00:00:35.959 --> 00:00:37.709
events we have in L.A.

00:00:38.875 --> 00:00:40.937
I grew up running around in the streets of

00:00:40.937 --> 00:00:43.169
Lynwood, Compton and South Gate.

00:00:43.169 --> 00:00:46.906
I got shot at a few times,
I got mugged a few times.

00:00:46.906 --> 00:00:49.723
If I didn't have music, and I
didn't have the ska punk shows

00:00:49.723 --> 00:00:53.890
to go to, I don't know
where I would've been.

00:00:54.089 --> 00:00:56.336
(uptempo music)

00:00:56.336 --> 00:01:00.503
Latin ska punk has influences
from all over the place.

00:01:00.726 --> 00:01:03.762
It's a genre that collects
bit and pieces of everything.

00:01:03.762 --> 00:01:07.115
From Jamaican reggae, to hardcore,

00:01:07.115 --> 00:01:09.282
to the Mexican, Latin ska.

00:01:10.123 --> 00:01:12.790
It's just a clash of everything.

00:01:14.944 --> 00:01:17.352
(latin ska music)

00:01:17.352 --> 00:01:21.519
Latin ska started popping up
in L.A. in the mid-late 90s.

00:01:21.842 --> 00:01:24.018
It was in the neighborhood.

00:01:24.018 --> 00:01:27.168
We had a hard time
playing legitimate venues.

00:01:27.168 --> 00:01:30.677
That's kind of what sparked
me having to organize

00:01:30.677 --> 00:01:34.202
some of these events, because
no one would do it for us.

00:01:34.202 --> 00:01:36.614
(slow dance music)

00:01:36.614 --> 00:01:38.975
I think a lot of these
Latino kids in neighborhoods,

00:01:38.975 --> 00:01:40.944
are attracted to ska music.

00:01:40.944 --> 00:01:43.579
The way people dance,
the way people dress,

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:45.390
they just find themselves in that.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.178
The rhythm really gets you going.

00:01:47.178 --> 00:01:49.436
It touches a lot of people.

00:01:49.436 --> 00:01:51.229
To a lot of these kids
that go to our shows,

00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:53.216
it helps them find themselves.

00:01:53.216 --> 00:01:55.786
It makes them feel like
they do belong to something,

00:01:55.786 --> 00:01:59.119
it's a very personal connection to them.

00:02:01.734 --> 00:02:04.151
(soft chime)

